<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Baker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Samuel White Baker</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Baker was an English explorer who in 1864, with his partner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a> and saw the waterfall he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murchison-falls">Murchison Falls</a>. From 1869 to 1873 he served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a> as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatoria">Equatoria</a>, trying to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and extend Egyptian rule south. In 1872 he proclaimed the annexation of Bunyoro at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masindi">Masindi</a> but was driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabalega">Kabalega</a>'s forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-baker.jpg" alt="Samuel Baker" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeonisRugitur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir-Baker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samuel White Baker was born on 8 June 1821 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> into a wealthy commercial family; his father was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> merchant, banker and shipowner. He completed his studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 1841. As a civil engineer he later planned railways and bridges across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. In 1846 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, where the next year he founded an agricultural settlement at Nuwara Eliya. His first wife, Henrietta, died of typhoid fever in 1855. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in 1858 and 1859 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, who became his wife and followed him on all his travels; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a> called her Anyadwe, Daughter of the Moon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In March 1861 Baker began his first tour of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in his own words to discover the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. After a year on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>–Ethiopian frontier he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> and in December 1862 set out up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>. At Gondokoro he met John Speke and James Grant, who gave him information that allowed him to make the first European visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a>, which he first sighted on 14 March 1864. He showed that the Nile flowed through the lake, though he overestimated its contribution to the river's flow. He and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> were also the first Europeans to see a substantial waterfall on the Victoria Nile, which he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murchison-falls">Murchison Falls</a> after Sir Roderick Murchison.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in October 1865, received the Patron's Medal of the Royal Geographical Society and was knighted in August 1866. In 1869, at the request of the khedive Ismail, he led a military expedition of 1,700 Egyptian troops to the equatorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and open the way to commerce. He was made a pasha and major-general and governor of the new territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatoria">Equatoria</a> for four years. He met the blocking of the river in the Sudd, hostility from officials who profited from the slave trade and armed opposition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a> succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Baker published his narrative of the expedition as Ismailia in 1874. He bought the Sandford Orleigh estate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> that year and died there of a heart attack on 30 December 1893. He never received quite the same acclaim as other British explorers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, in part because of the circumstances of his marriage and a scandal involving his brother Valentine. Baker's Falls in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and Mount Baker in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rwenzori-mountains">Rwenzori Mountains</a> bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Baker” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1872 he drove off <b>Samuel Baker</b>, who had tried to annex Bunyoro for Egypt, after fighting at Masindi, and in 1876 he reconquered Toro.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Newton Abbot, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1867" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel Baker"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
